## Summary

The `bash-poke` pi extension currently lives as a stopgap at `~/.pi/agent/extensions/bash-poke.ts`, outside the repo. It works, but it is intentionally rudimentary. It should become a proper workspace package under `package/pi-plugin/`, and the promotion is an opportunity to improve it: the current implementation can and should be improved, not ported verbatim.

## What the stopgap does

- `!cmd` takes over execution via pi's `user_bash` extension event, returns control to the editor immediately (a placeholder is recorded in context), and sends the model a poke message with the real output when the process exits.
- `!!cmd` (hidden) is passed through untouched.
- Poke message: `[bash finished] $ cmd (exit N)` plus the fenced output capped at 8000 chars plus the instruction `continue`, sent via `pi.sendUserMessage(text, { deliverAs: "followUp" })` so it fires immediately when idle and queues behind current work otherwise.
- `/poke` lists background commands, `/poke cancel` aborts them; cancelled commands do not poke.
- Concurrency is allowed; native `!` rejects a second command while one runs.

Background: native pi `!` is foreground-blocking as of 0.85.1. The TUI submit handler warns `A bash command is already running. Press Esc to cancel it first.` and awaits completion. Taking over execution through `user_bash` is what buys background semantics; the motivating use case is `! sleep 7200` as a delayed `continue` after a provider usage-limit reset.

## Verified API facts

All verified against the installed `@earendil-works/pi-coding-agent` 0.85.1 dist, so implementation needs no rediscovery.

- `user_bash` handler returns `UserBashEventResult` (`{ operations?: BashOperations, result?: BashResult }`). First non-undefined handler result wins; returning nothing falls back to native execution. See `dist/core/extensions/types.d.ts`.
- Returning `result` replaces execution entirely and loses live output streaming: pi renders the returned `BashResult` once, after the handler resolves. The `operations` path keeps pi driving execution (streaming chunks, abort signal, truncation) and is the promising route for recovering streaming.
- `pi.exec(command, args, { cwd, signal })` has no default timeout and resolves with `{ stdout, stderr, code, killed }`. See `dist/core/exec.js`.
- `pi.sendUserMessage(content, { deliverAs: "followUp" })` always triggers a turn; while streaming it queues after all work.
- `registerCommand` handlers receive `(args: string, ctx)`; `ctx.ui.notify(message, type?)` is guarded by `ctx.hasUI`.

## Known limitations to improve

- No live output streaming. Possibly recoverable via the `operations` path instead of `result` replacement.
- Hard-coded constants (`POKE_INSTRUCTION = "continue"`, `MAX_POKE_CHARS = 8000`) instead of settings.
- No interaction with usage-limit state: a poke that lands before the limit resets wastes a failed turn with no retry ladder.
- The interface itself is worth rethinking (the stopgap's author considers it rudimentary); e.g. how the pending state, cancellation, and poke content should surface.
- No tests, README, `mise.toml`, or logging. Repo standards apply: tagged loggers via `@monochromatic-dev/module-logger`, tests covering every exported code path.

## Target shape

Mirror `package/pi-plugin/agent-settled-notification`: directory `package/pi-plugin/bash-poke`, package name `@monochromatic-dev/pi-plugin-bash-poke`, `pi.extensions` pointing at the built bundle, `mise.toml` tasks mirroring siblings, README, lint and test tasks green.

```json
// package/pi-plugin/bash-poke/package.json (excerpt)
{
  "name": "@monochromatic-dev/pi-plugin-bash-poke",
  "pi": { "extensions": ["./dist/final/node/index.mjs"] },
  "peerDependencies": { "@earendil-works/pi-coding-agent": "*" }
}
```

## Reference implementation

The current working source, for porting (move it into the repo rather than leaving it in personal dotfiles):

